content.text:
The essay is written and saved as `./output/go-first.md`, the only file in that folder: about 2,000 words of essay plus a source list, 15 KB, no raw HTML or images.

**What it argues.** I chose one idea rather than a list of virtues: people consistently underestimate how honest, helpful, welcoming and like-minded other people are. Because everyone reads everyone else's silence as indifference, that mistake keeps itself going. The essay opens and closes in a classroom where nobody raises a hand, and its one request is to go first.

**Why this topic.** It fits the "if everyone read it" premise unusually well. A false belief that survives only because everyone holds it privately is the kind of problem that could be fixed by everyone reading the same correction at once.

**Evidence.** The claims rest on published studies, including:
- the 40-country lost-wallet experiment;
- the 125-country climate survey (69% willing to contribute, while guessing 43% of others would);
- the Riyadh study on women working outside the home;
- security-camera research on bystanders stepping in;
- the end of foot-binding through public pledges.

I checked every DOI against Crossref. I checked the climate, loneliness and social-media figures, and the World Happiness Report wallet claim, against their source pages; the remaining study details are written from memory of the papers. The essay also includes a section on what it is not claiming, such as that everyone is good or that speaking up is safe everywhere.

Blurb: Go First is an essay arguing that almost everyone underestimates the honesty, helpfulness and goodwill of almost everyone else, and that this shared mistake keeps people silent, lonely and stuck with customs nobody wants. Drawing on research from dozens of countries, it asks each reader to make the first move, because their silence is what other people are reading as evidence.
